/// Particle introspect component world.
///
/// Build-time helper. Phase 5 of the build pipeline: the orchestrator
/// instantiates this component with the freshly bundled JS mounted as a
/// virtual filesystem, then calls `manifest()` to extract the particle's
/// manifest from the bundle's default export.
///
/// Distinct from `particle-runtime.wasm` because the boot/serve logic is
/// fundamentally different — introspect never invokes a tool handler. It
/// imports the bundle, reads the metadata side of the default export,
/// and serializes it. Splitting it out lets each component evolve
/// independently and keeps the hot-path runtime image free of build-time
/// logic.
///
/// Crucially, this component does NOT import the `particle:host/*`
/// capability interfaces. The user's bundle still contains
/// `import { credentials } from "particle:credentials"` (etc.), but
/// since handlers are never called, those modules are never *used* —
/// only loaded. The introspect component's JS source provides local
/// no-op stubs for every `particle:*` module before evaluating the
/// bundle, so module resolution succeeds without crossing any WIT
/// boundary. The host only wires the standard wasi:* imports.
///
/// Spec: docs/initial-design.md §3 (`particle-introspect.wasm`).
package particle:introspect@0.1.0;

interface introspect {
  variant introspect-error {
    bundle-load-error(string),
    invalid-manifest(string),
  }

  /// Returns the manifest as JSON: { name, description, version,
  /// capabilities, tools: [{ name, description, inputSchema }] }.
  manifest: func() -> result<string, introspect-error>;
}

world component {
  // No particle:host/* imports. See the package doc above for why.
  // wasi:* imports are added implicitly by wasm-rquickjs based on which
  // JS APIs the source uses (fetch, console, node:fs, etc.).
  export introspect;
}
